"incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 15.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 6.87, "baseline_margin": 12.44, "analyst_adjustment": 0.56, "key_factors": ["Colorado's D+6 lean and favorable midterm conditions give Democrats a double-digit starting edge", "All seven expert ratings call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and markets price about 97% for Weiser", "Victor Marx's record campaign-finance fine and unconventional candidacy limit Republican competitiveness", "Open seat with no public general-election polling leaves above-average uncertainty"], "rationale": "Colorado's Democratic lean and favorable midterm conditions give Phil Weiser a double-digit edge, every major rater calls the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and betting markets price his chances near 97%. Victor Marx's record campaign-finance fine and unconventional profile have kept the race from becoming competitive, though the open seat and absence of polling keep uncertainty elevated.", "watch": "The election-office investigation of Weiser or consolidation of anti-Weiser voters behind Marx and independent Greg Lopez could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Jared Polis (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Colorado's governor race is open after two terms of Jared Polis, and Democrats start with a clear edge. Attorney General Phil Weiser carries a state that has leaned about six points Democratic in recent federal races, and midterm conditions add to that advantage. Republican nominee Victor Marx has struggled: he was hit with a record campaign-finance fine of roughly $58,000, and coverage has centered on his unusual biography rather than a governing agenda.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the picture rests on the state's partisan lean, expert ratings and markets. Every major race rater calls Colorado Solid or Safe Democratic, and betting markets put